// GENERATED lesson content. Short lesson + longer 'explain' text.
class Lesson {
  final String area, title, short, action, explain;
  const Lesson(this.area, this.title, this.short, this.action, this.explain);
  String get id => '$area:$title';
}

const lessons = <Lesson>[
  Lesson("physical", "Sleep is the foundation", "Most adults need 7–9 hours. Sleep is when your body repairs and your mind locks in what you learned.", "Pick one fixed wake-up time and keep it for a week.", "A steady wake time anchors your body clock even more than bedtime does. Dim lights and screens an hour before bed, keep the room cool and dark, and limit caffeine after midday. One short night will not hurt, but weeks of short sleep affect mood, focus, appetite and recovery. If you regularly cannot sleep despite good habits, talk with a doctor."),
  Lesson("physical", "Move a little every day", "Daily movement beats the occasional heroic workout.", "Walk for 15 minutes today.", "Public health guidance suggests about 150 minutes of moderate activity a week plus strength work twice a week, roughly 20 to 30 minutes a day. Start smaller than feels useful: a short walk you actually do beats a plan you skip. Attach it to something you already do, like right after lunch."),
  Lesson("physical", "Strength protects your future", "Muscle and bone strength keep you independent and injury-free for decades.", "Do one bodyweight workout from the Fitness tab this week.", "Strength training twice a week builds muscle, supports bones, improves balance and helps blood sugar. You do not need a gym: squats, push-ups, rows and planks go far. Use good form, add reps or difficulty slowly over weeks, and rest a day between hard sessions for the same muscles."),
  Lesson("physical", "Eat simply", "Mostly whole foods, enough protein, plenty of plants, and water.", "Add one vegetable or fruit to your next meal.", "Rules that last are simple: build most meals around vegetables, protein and fiber-rich foods, drink water, and treat sweets and fast food as occasional rather than forbidden. Strict diets often backfire; adding good things works better than banning things. If you have a medical condition or a history of disordered eating, ask a professional before changing how you eat."),
  Lesson("physical", "Build habits that stick", "Make it tiny, tie it to a cue, and celebrate right away.", "Choose one two-minute habit and attach it to something you do daily.", "Habits form through repetition in a steady context, not willpower. Shrink the habit until it is almost too easy (one push-up, one glass of water), link it to an existing cue (\"after I brush my teeth\"), and notice the small win. Grow it later. If you miss a day, just resume; the one to avoid is missing twice in a row."),
  Lesson("physical", "Rest and recover", "Rest is part of getting stronger, not a break from it.", "Plan one full rest evening this week.", "Muscles, tendons and your nervous system adapt during recovery, not during the effort. Signs you need more rest: constant fatigue, nagging aches, poor sleep and falling motivation. Sleep, gentle movement and time off hard training help. Rest is mental too, so leave some time unscheduled."),
  Lesson("physical", "Calm your body under stress", "Slow breathing and movement settle your body fast.", "Do the one-minute Calm timer.", "Stress shows up in the body: tight shoulders, shallow breath, a racing heart. Breathing out longer than you breathe in, such as 4 in and 6 out, activates the body's calming response. Walks, stretching and time outside also ease tension. These help everyday stress; ongoing anxiety deserves professional support."),
  Lesson("physical", "Prevention beats repair", "Check-ups, dental visits and screenings catch problems early.", "Book one overdue appointment.", "Many conditions are easier to treat when found early. Keep up with routine check-ups, dental care, vaccines and screenings recommended for your age, and know your family history. If cost is a barrier, community clinics and local health departments often offer low-cost options. This is general education, not medical advice."),
  Lesson("intellectual", "Learn how you learn", "Practicing recall beats re-reading. Testing yourself builds memory.", "After reading something, close it and write what you remember.", "Research on learning shows retrieval practice (recalling from memory) and spaced repetition (reviewing over growing gaps) outperform rereading and highlighting. It feels harder, and that difficulty is what makes it work. Try summarizing from memory, flashcards, or explaining it out loud."),
  Lesson("intellectual", "Teach it to learn it", "If you can explain it simply, you understand it.", "Explain something from this week to someone, or in three sentences.", "Explaining forces you to find the gaps in your understanding. Imagine teaching a curious ten-year-old: plain words, concrete examples. Wherever you stumble is what to study next. This is often called the Feynman technique."),
  Lesson("intellectual", "Read a little every day", "Ten pages a day is about a book a month.", "Read ten minutes tonight instead of scrolling.", "Small daily reading adds up. Choose books you actually want to finish, quit the ones you do not, and mix fiction with nonfiction. Jot one takeaway per session. Audiobooks count if you give them your attention."),
  Lesson("intellectual", "Practice deliberately", "Short, focused work on the hard part beats long, casual repetition.", "Spend 20 focused minutes on one specific weak spot.", "Deliberate practice means a clear goal, full attention, quick feedback and working just beyond your comfort zone. Break a skill into pieces, drill the hardest piece, and get feedback from a teacher, a recording or your results. Twenty minutes of this daily outweighs hours of drifting."),
  Lesson("intellectual", "Protect your focus", "Deep thinking needs uninterrupted time.", "Put your phone in another room for a 25-minute focus session.", "Constant notifications split your attention, and every switch costs time to refocus. Work in blocks of 25 to 50 minutes on one task, phone out of reach, then take a short break. The focus timer on your goals is built for this."),
  Lesson("intellectual", "Be curious on purpose", "Questions drive learning, so keep a list of what you wonder about.", "Write down three things you are curious about and look one up.", "Curiosity is a habit. When something makes you wonder, jot it down and research it later. Follow interesting threads, talk to people who know more, and get comfortable saying \"I do not know yet.\""),
  Lesson("intellectual", "Think clearly", "Check your reasoning: what is the evidence, and what else could explain it?", "Next time you hear a strong claim, ask how you would know if it were wrong.", "Everyone has biases, like favoring information that agrees with us. Slow down on big claims, check the source, look for the strongest opposing view, and notice when emotion is driving a conclusion. Being willing to change your mind is a strength."),
  Lesson("intellectual", "Make learning a plan", "A small written learning goal beats a vague wish.", "Pick one skill and set a 30-day goal with a weekly step.", "Be specific: not \"learn to code\" but \"build a small page that shows my name.\" Break it into weekly steps, schedule the time and track it. Libraries, free tutorials and community classes make most subjects reachable. Tell someone your goal for accountability."),
  Lesson("emotional", "Name what you feel", "A precise word for a feeling lowers its intensity.", "Finish this sentence: \"I feel ___ because ___.\"", "Labeling emotions engages the thinking part of your brain and often calms the reaction. Go beyond \"bad\": disappointed, anxious, lonely, overwhelmed, embarrassed. Feelings are information, not orders. You can notice anger without acting on it."),
  Lesson("emotional", "Pause before reacting", "There is a small gap between trigger and response. Use it.", "Next time you are upset, take three slow breaths before replying.", "A pause gives your calmer thinking time to catch up. Breathe out slowly, drop your shoulders, and delay the text or the argument. \"Let me think about that and get back to you\" is a perfectly good sentence."),
  Lesson("emotional", "Talk to yourself kindly", "You would not speak to a friend the way you speak to yourself.", "Catch one harsh thought today and reword it as you would to a friend.", "Self-criticism rarely motivates in the long run, while self-compassion supports resilience and trying again. Notice the thought, ask whether it is true and useful, and replace it with something accurate and kind: \"I messed up, and I can fix it.\""),
  Lesson("emotional", "Handle stress in the moment", "Slow breathing, movement and grounding calm you quickly.", "Do the one-minute Calm timer now.", "Try breathing out longer than in, naming five things you can see, or taking a short walk. Over time, sleep, exercise, time with people and fewer overcommitments lower your baseline stress. If stress feels constant or unmanageable, reach out for support."),
  Lesson("emotional", "Keep a feelings journal", "A few lines a day reveal your patterns.", "Write three sentences in tonight's journal.", "Writing about what happened and how you felt helps you process it and spot triggers. Do not aim for perfect: note what happened, how you felt and what you needed. Look back over the week in your weekly review."),
  Lesson("emotional", "Bounce back from setbacks", "Resilience is built by recovering, not by never falling.", "Write one thing you learned from a recent setback.", "Setbacks are normal and temporary. Ask what is in your control, what you can learn and what small next step is possible. Lean on people, keep your routines, and avoid reading one failure as proof about your whole life."),
  Lesson("emotional", "Set healthy boundaries", "Saying no to some things lets you say yes to what matters.", "Decline or postpone one thing you do not have energy for.", "A boundary is a calm, clear statement of what you will and will not do: \"I cannot take that on right now.\" You do not owe a long excuse. People who respect you will adjust, and protecting your energy helps you care for others sustainably."),
  Lesson("emotional", "Know when to ask for help", "Reaching out to a friend, counselor or doctor is strength.", "Tell one trusted person how you have really been.", "If low mood, anxiety or worry lasts for weeks or gets in the way of daily life, talk with a doctor or licensed counselor. If you ever feel unsafe or think about hurting yourself, call or text 988 (US), see findahelpline.com for other countries, or contact your local emergency number. Reaching out early helps."),
  Lesson("social", "Listen to understand", "Most people want to feel heard more than advised.", "In your next conversation, ask one follow-up question before sharing your own story.", "Active listening: put the phone down, make eye contact, ask open questions, reflect back what you heard (\"that sounds frustrating\"), and resist fixing. People remember how heard they felt."),
  Lesson("social", "Show up consistently", "Relationships grow through small, regular contact.", "Send a friend a quick \"thinking of you.\"", "Friendships rarely end in conflict; they fade from silence. Short, regular touchpoints like a text, a call or a shared walk matter more than rare grand gestures. Put recurring catch-ups on your calendar."),
  Lesson("social", "Start conversations", "Curious questions beat small talk.", "Ask someone, \"What are you looking forward to?\"", "Open questions that start with what, how or tell me about invite real answers. Use their reply to ask another. Be okay with awkward starts, because most people welcome genuine interest. The Connect tab has question ideas."),
  Lesson("social", "Be honest and kind", "Say hard things clearly and gently.", "Think of one thing left unsaid and write how you would say it kindly.", "Use \"I\" statements: \"I felt hurt when...\" instead of \"You always...\". Choose a calm moment, describe the behavior, share your feeling and make a request. Honesty with care builds trust."),
  Lesson("social", "Repair conflict", "Conflict is normal. Repair is what matters.", "If tension is lingering, send a short, sincere message to reset.", "A good apology names what you did, acknowledges the impact and says what you will do differently, without a \"but.\" Seek to understand their view first. Not every relationship can be repaired, but a sincere try is worth it."),
  Lesson("social", "Choose your circle", "You tend to become like the people you spend the most time with.", "List the three people who lift you most and spend time with one this week.", "Invest in people who are supportive, honest and kind, and limit time with those who consistently drain or belittle you. Quality beats quantity: a few close relationships protect well-being more than many shallow ones."),
  Lesson("social", "Be there for family and partners", "Attention and appreciation keep close relationships healthy.", "Tell someone close one specific thing you appreciate about them.", "Small daily moments of attention build closeness: a real greeting, asking about their day, undivided time. Express appreciation specifically. In romantic relationships, respect, communication and shared time matter; in any relationship, safety and respect are non-negotiable."),
  Lesson("social", "Build new connections", "New friendships come from repeated, shared time.", "Join or attend one group, class or event this month.", "Friendship usually forms through repeated time together, so join something recurring: a club, class, team, volunteer group or faith or community gathering. Say yes to invitations and follow up with a message. It takes time, so keep showing up."),
  Lesson("work", "Know where your money goes", "You cannot manage what you cannot see.", "Total last month's income and spending.", "List income, fixed bills and variable spending for one month. A notebook or an app both work. The goal is awareness, not guilt. Spot one leak to fix. This is general education, not financial advice."),
  Lesson("work", "Pay yourself first", "Save automatically before you spend.", "Set up an automatic transfer to savings, even a small one.", "Saving is easier when it is automatic. Even \$10 to \$25 each payday builds the habit, and you can raise it over time. Treat savings like a bill you always pay."),
  Lesson("work", "Build a safety cushion", "An emergency fund turns crises into inconveniences.", "Set a first goal, like \$500 or one week of expenses.", "Aim eventually for three to six months of essential expenses in an accessible account, but start with a small first target. It reduces stress and keeps you out of high-interest debt when surprises happen."),
  Lesson("work", "Handle debt wisely", "High-interest debt costs the most, so tackle it on purpose.", "List each debt with its interest rate and minimum payment.", "Pay minimums on everything, then put extra toward either the highest interest rate (saves the most money) or the smallest balance (quick wins), whichever keeps you going. Avoid new high-interest debt. Nonprofit credit counseling can help if you feel stuck."),
  Lesson("work", "Understand how money grows", "Time and compounding reward starting early.", "Learn what an index fund or retirement account is where you live.", "Interest earning interest grows money over decades. Employer retirement plans, especially with a match, are worth understanding. Investing involves risk: learn the basics from reputable sources and consider a licensed professional for personal decisions."),
  Lesson("work", "Grow your career", "Skills, relationships and visibility open doors.", "Spend 30 minutes updating your resume or learning a work skill.", "Know what you want from work, keep a running list of your accomplishments, ask for feedback and learn skills that are in demand. Build a network by helping others. Review your direction every few months."),
  Lesson("work", "Ask for what you are worth", "Prepare, be specific, and ask.", "Write down your accomplishments and the number you would ask for.", "Research typical pay for your role and location, list your results, practice what you will say, and ask calmly and directly. The worst outcome is usually \"not now,\" and you learn what to work on."),
  Lesson("work", "Find stability and purpose in work", "Aim for work that fits your values, strengths and life.", "Write what matters most in a job: pay, growth, flexibility, meaning.", "Few jobs are perfect. Clarify your priorities and see how your current work measures up. Small changes, like a new project, a conversation with your manager or a side skill, can help before a big move. Protect time away from work too."),
  Lesson("spiritual", "Clarify your values", "Values are what matters most to you, and they guide your choices.", "Write your top three values, for example honesty, family, growth.", "Scan a list of values and circle the ones that feel essential, then narrow to three. Ask where your time and money actually go and whether that matches. The gap shows where to adjust."),
  Lesson("spiritual", "Find your purpose", "Purpose is a direction, not a single answer.", "Finish this sentence: \"I want my life to help ___.\"", "Purpose often comes from combining what you care about, what you are good at and what others need. It can be small, like raising kind kids or improving things at work, and it evolves. Try things and notice what energizes you."),
  Lesson("spiritual", "Practice stillness", "A few quiet minutes a day clear the mind.", "Sit in silence for five minutes with no phone.", "Stillness can be meditation, prayer, breathing or simply sitting. Notice your thoughts without chasing them and return to your breath or a word. Short, regular practice builds steadiness and perspective. Do what fits your own beliefs."),
  Lesson("spiritual", "Practice gratitude", "Noticing good things changes what you see.", "Write three specific things you are grateful for tonight.", "Specific beats general: not \"my family\" but \"my sister called when I was down.\" Gratitude supports mood and relationships. Tell the people involved, too."),
  Lesson("spiritual", "Serve something bigger", "Helping others gives life meaning.", "Do one kind thing for someone who cannot repay you.", "Volunteering, giving, mentoring or simply helping a neighbor connects you to something beyond yourself. Start small and local. Meaning often comes from contribution."),
  Lesson("spiritual", "Spend time in nature", "Time outdoors puts life in perspective.", "Take a 15-minute walk outside without headphones.", "Nature lowers stress and invites wonder. Notice trees, sky, weather and sounds. Awe, the feeling of something vast, is linked with humility and well-being."),
  Lesson("spiritual", "Examine your beliefs", "Thoughtful beliefs are held, tested and sometimes revised.", "Write one belief you hold and why you hold it.", "Whether religious, philosophical or secular, ask where your beliefs came from, what supports them and how they shape your actions. Read and listen to thoughtful people who see it differently. Respect other people's paths and stay true to your own."),
  Lesson("spiritual", "Live by your values", "Integrity means your actions match your words.", "At the end of the day ask: \"Did I act on my values today?\"", "Make one small decision each day with your values in mind and notice when you drift. Forgive slips, repair them and adjust. A life of small aligned choices adds up to a meaningful one."),
];
